const WH={
1:['A nurse claims the average recovery time is 5 days. A researcher suspects it is actually longer. Which pair of hypotheses fits this situation?~H₀: μ = 5, H₁: μ ≠ 5~H₀: μ = 5, H₁: μ > 5~H₀: μ > 5, H₁: μ = 5~1~"Longer" is a one-directional claim, so H₁ uses > and the test is right-tailed.','Why must H₀ and H₁ always be written before the data is collected?~So the decision is not influenced by results already seen~So the sample size can be skipped~So α becomes unnecessary~0~Setting the hypotheses first keeps the test objective and prevents bias.','A factory owner is accused of underpaying workers below the legal minimum. Which hypothesis pair tests this claim?~H₀: μ = minimum, H₁: μ < minimum~H₀: μ = minimum, H₁: μ > minimum~H₀: μ ≠ minimum, H₁: μ = minimum~0~"Below" the minimum is a left-tailed claim, so H₁ uses <.','A jury tries a defendant assuming innocence until proven guilty. Which part of hypothesis testing does this resemble?~H₀, which is rejected only with strong evidence~H₁, which needs no evidence~α, which has no role in decisions~0~H₀ is treated as true by default, just like the presumption of innocence, until evidence forces a rejection.','A researcher wrongly concludes that a safe drug is dangerous when it is actually safe. Which error is this?~Type I error~Type II error~No error occurred~0~Rejecting a true H₀ (the drug is safe) is a Type I error.','A quality inspector fails to detect a real defect rate increase, wrongly concluding production is normal. Which error is this?~Type I error~Type II error~This is a correct decision~1~Failing to reject a false H₀ is a Type II error — a missed real effect.','Which situation would you expect to use a two-tailed test?~Testing if a new fertilizer changes (up or down) average crop yield~Testing if a diet only increases weight loss~Testing if a machine only produces fewer defects~0~"Changes" without a stated direction calls for H₁: μ ≠ value, a two-tailed test.','A company wants strong proof before switching suppliers, accepting only a 1% chance of a wrong "switch" decision. What does this describe?~Choosing a small α to reduce Type I error risk~Choosing a large α to speed up the decision~Ignoring α since it does not matter here~0~A 1% tolerance for a wrong rejection of H₀ is a small, strict α = 0.01.','If a test result could plausibly be explained by random chance alone, what should the researcher most likely do?~Fail to reject H₀~Reject H₀ and claim proof~Change the data until it fits~0~When chance alone plausibly explains the result, there is not enough evidence to reject H₀.','A school claims average test scores "improved" this year compared to a fixed historical average. How should H₁ be written?~H₁: μ > historical average~H₁: μ < historical average~H₁: μ = historical average~0~"Improved" implies an increase, so H₁ uses > and the test is right-tailed.'],
2:['A researcher wants very strong evidence before rejecting H₀ in a medical study. Which α should they choose?~α = 0.01~α = 0.10~α = 0.20~0~A smaller α demands stronger evidence before rejecting H₀.','Why is α chosen before the test is run rather than after seeing the result?~To prevent the researcher from adjusting the standard to fit the outcome~Because α cannot change the decision~Because α is only used for two-tailed tests~0~Fixing α beforehand keeps the level of significance from being manipulated after seeing results.','A test states H₁: μ > 50. Where should you expect the rejection region on the distribution?~In the right tail only~In the left tail only~Split between both tails~0~A ">" alternative places the rejection region entirely in the right tail.','A two-tailed test uses α = 0.10. How much area falls in each tail?~0.05 in each tail~0.10 in each tail~0.20 in each tail~0~A two-tailed α is split equally, so each tail gets α/2 = 0.05.','A computed Z = 1.80 is compared to a critical value of ±1.96 in a two-tailed test at α = 0.05. What should the researcher conclude?~Fail to reject H₀, since 1.80 does not exceed 1.96~Reject H₀, since 1.80 is a large number~Cannot decide without more data~0~1.80 falls inside the non-rejection region between −1.96 and 1.96.','A p-value of 0.03 is obtained at α = 0.05. What is the correct decision?~Reject H₀, since p ≤ α~Fail to reject H₀, since p is small~Increase α to match p~0~When p is less than or equal to α, the result is statistically significant, so H₀ is rejected.','If a researcher lowers α from 0.05 to 0.01 without changing anything else, what happens to the chance of rejecting H₀?~It becomes harder to reject H₀~It becomes easier to reject H₀~Nothing changes~0~A smaller α narrows the rejection region, requiring more extreme evidence to reject H₀.','Why do researchers avoid saying "we accept H₀" even after failing to reject it?~Because failing to reject only means insufficient evidence, not proof H₀ is true~Because H₀ is always false~Because α becomes 1 in that case~0~A non-significant result does not prove H₀; it only shows the evidence was not strong enough.','A study claims a coaching program changes test scores, without specifying direction. Which type of test and rejection region should be used?~Two-tailed, with regions in both tails~Right-tailed, with the region in the right tail only~Left-tailed, with the region in the left tail only~0~"Changes" with no stated direction requires a two-tailed H₁ and rejection regions in both tails.','A test at α = 0.05 (two-tailed) gives a Z statistic beyond both critical values. What can be said about the p-value?~The p-value is less than 0.05~The p-value is greater than 0.05~The p-value equals 1~0~A statistic beyond the critical values corresponds to a p-value smaller than α.'],
3:['A sample of n = 36 has x̄ = 52, μ₀ = 50, σ = 6. What is the correct next step after computing Z?~Compare |Z| with the critical value to decide on H₀~Immediately conclude H₁ is proven~Change μ₀ to match x̄~0~The computed Z is compared against the critical value to make the reject/fail-to-reject decision.','Two samples give the same difference between x̄ and μ₀, but one has a much larger n. What happens to |Z|?~|Z| becomes larger for the larger sample~|Z| becomes smaller for the larger sample~|Z| stays exactly the same~0~A larger n shrinks the standard error, which increases |Z| for the same difference.','Why would a researcher use a t-test instead of a Z-test?~Because the population standard deviation σ is unknown and estimated by s~Because the sample size is very large and σ is known~Because α is set to 0.10~0~t accounts for the added uncertainty of estimating σ from the sample.','A computed Z = 2.50 exceeds the critical value of 1.96. What should the final written conclusion say?~There is enough evidence, at this α, that the population mean differs from μ₀~The population mean is exactly x̄~No conclusion can be written~0~Rejecting H₀ means the sample gives enough evidence, at the chosen α, to say the mean differs.','A test yields Z = 1.10 with a critical value of 1.96. What is the most appropriate conclusion?~There is not enough evidence to say the mean differs from μ₀~The mean is definitely equal to μ₀~The test was performed incorrectly~0~Failing to reject H₀ means insufficient evidence, not proof of equality.','If the standard error of the mean is halved while the difference x̄ − μ₀ stays the same, what happens to Z?~Z doubles~Z is cut in half~Z becomes zero~0~Z = (x̄ − μ₀)/SE, so halving SE doubles Z for the same numerator.','A company retests a claim with a much larger sample and gets a bigger Z for the same observed difference. What explains this?~A larger sample reduces standard error, making the same difference more significant~The company changed α~The formula for Z changed~0~Larger samples produce smaller standard errors, magnifying the same raw difference into a larger Z.','Why is stating "reject H₀" alone considered an incomplete conclusion in applied research?~Because it should be explained in the context of the real-world problem~Because H₀ should never be rejected~Because Z values are never reported~0~A complete conclusion translates the statistical decision into the meaning for the actual research question.','A hospital wants to detect even a small shift in average recovery time. Which sample size choice better supports this goal?~A larger sample size, since it increases the test’s ability to detect small differences~A smaller sample size, since it is faster~Sample size does not affect this at all~0~Larger samples reduce standard error and make the test more sensitive to smaller true differences.','A test statistic falls exactly on the critical value boundary. What is the most defensible next step?~Report the exact p-value and use it, since falling exactly on the boundary is ambiguous by the critical-value rule alone~Always fail to reject regardless of p-value~Always reject regardless of p-value~0~Boundary cases are best resolved by examining the exact p-value rather than the approximate critical-value cutoff.'],
4:['Two variables have r = 0.92. What can be concluded about their relationship?~A strong positive linear relationship~A weak linear relationship~No relationship at all~0~r close to +1 indicates a strong positive linear association.','A study finds r = −0.85 between hours of screen time and sleep duration. What does this suggest?~As screen time increases, sleep duration tends to strongly decrease~As screen time increases, sleep duration also increases~Screen time causes less sleep~0~A strong negative r means the variables move in opposite directions; it does not prove causation.','A news article claims "ice cream sales cause drowning deaths" because both rise together in summer. What is the best statistical criticism?~A third variable, hot weather, likely explains both, so correlation does not imply causation~The correlation must be negative~r cannot be computed for this data~0~A confounding variable can produce correlation between two variables that do not cause each other.','Which value of r indicates the weakest linear relationship?~r = 0.05~r = 0.60~r = −0.90~0~r near 0 shows the weakest linear association regardless of sign.','A researcher reports r = 0.40 and claims it proves a meaningful cause-and-effect relationship. What is the flaw in this claim?~Correlation, even if moderate, does not by itself establish causation~r = 0.40 is actually a perfect correlation~r cannot be positive~0~Even a nontrivial correlation only shows association, not that one variable causes the other.','Which pair of variables would you expect to show a strong positive correlation?~Hours studied and exam score~Shoe size and exam score~Favorite color and exam score~0~Study time and exam performance typically move together, unlike unrelated variables.','A scatter plot shows points tightly clustered along a downward line. What does this suggest about r?~r is close to −1~r is close to +1~r is close to 0~0~A tight downward pattern indicates a strong negative linear relationship.','Why can’t Pearson’s r be used to describe a strong curved (non-linear) relationship?~Because r only measures the strength of a linear pattern~Because r is always 0 for curved data~Because curved data has no variables~0~Pearson’s r specifically captures linear association and can understate a strong non-linear pattern.','A dataset shows r = 1.00 between minutes studied and points earned on a 10-point quiz. What does this specifically mean?~Every data point lies exactly on a single ascending line~The two variables are unrelated~The quiz has 100 points~0~r = 1 means a perfect positive linear relationship, with no scatter around the line.','A guidance counselor wants to predict which two variables are most worth tracking together. Which r value between them signals the most useful linear relationship?~r = 0.88~r = 0.10~r = −0.02~0~The value closest to ±1 in magnitude indicates the strongest, most useful linear relationship.'],
5:['A regression line is Y = 3X + 20, where X is hours studied and Y is exam score. What does the value 20 represent?~The predicted score when a student studies 0 hours~The score increase per hour studied~The maximum possible score~0~The intercept (b) is the predicted Y when X = 0.','In Y = 3X + 20, what happens to the predicted score for each additional hour studied?~It increases by 3 points~It increases by 20 points~It decreases by 3 points~0~The slope (m = 3) is the change in Y for a one-unit increase in X.','A student studies 6 hours. Using Y = 3X + 20, what is the predicted exam score?~38~23~18~0~Y = 3(6) + 20 = 38.','Why is it risky to use a line of best fit built from students who studied 0–10 hours to predict the score for a student who studied 40 hours?~That is extrapolation beyond the observed data, where the linear trend may not hold~Because slope cannot be computed past 10 hours~Because the intercept becomes negative~0~Predicting far outside the range of the original data risks inaccurate extrapolation.','A regression line has a negative slope between price and quantity sold. What does this indicate?~As price increases, predicted quantity sold decreases~As price increases, predicted quantity sold increases~Price has no effect on sales~0~A negative slope means the response variable decreases as the explanatory variable increases.','Which axis should hold the explanatory (predictor) variable in a scatter plot?~The X axis~The Y axis~Either axis, it does not matter~0~By convention, the explanatory variable is plotted on X and the response on Y.','A scatter plot of the data shows a clear curve rather than a straight-line pattern. What is the most appropriate action?~Reconsider using a straight line of best fit, since linear regression assumes a linear trend~Force a straight line anyway with no changes~Ignore the scatter plot entirely~0~Linear regression assumes the underlying relationship is linear, so a curved pattern signals a poor fit.','Two regression lines both have slope 5, but one has a higher intercept. What does that difference mean graphically?~The line with the higher intercept is shifted upward, parallel to the other~The line with the higher intercept is steeper~The two lines must cross at X = 0~0~Changing only the intercept shifts the line vertically without changing its steepness.','A weather analyst uses temperature to predict ice cream sales with a strong linear pattern. Which use of the regression line is most appropriate?~Predicting sales at temperatures within the range already observed~Predicting sales at a temperature never recorded in any season~Predicting sales with no temperature data at all~0~Interpolation within the observed data range is a safer use of a fitted regression line than extrapolation.','A line of best fit has a very small slope close to 0. What does this suggest about the relationship between X and Y?~Y barely changes as X changes~Y changes dramatically as X changes~X and Y are perfectly correlated~0~A slope near 0 means little to no linear change in Y as X increases.']};
Object.keys(WH).forEach(k=>WH[k]=WH[k].map(P));
const Q6=['A researcher assumes innocence of H₀ until strong evidence appears. Which symbol represents this default assumption?~H₀~H₁~α~r~0~H₀ is the default, presumed-true statement.','A drug trial wrongly declares an ineffective drug effective. What error occurred?~Type I error~Type II error~Correct decision~No error~0~A false positive — rejecting a true H₀ — is a Type I error.','A safety inspector misses a real rise in defect rates, wrongly calling production normal. What error occurred?~Type I error~Type II error~Correct decision~No error~1~Failing to reject a false H₀ is a Type II error.','A company claims its battery lasts "more than 10 hours." How should H₁ be written?~μ = 10~μ > 10~μ < 10~μ ≠ 10~1~"More than" is a right-tailed, one-directional claim.','A study tests whether a new policy "changes" commute times, without stating a direction. What kind of test fits this?~Right-tailed~Left-tailed~Two-tailed~No test needed~2~An undirected "changes" claim requires a two-tailed H₁: μ ≠ value.','Why must α be fixed before running the test rather than adjusted afterward?~To keep the decision objective and unbiased~Because α cannot be a decimal~Because α always equals 0.05~Because α determines the sample size~0~Fixing α beforehand prevents the standard from being tailored to fit the results.','A two-tailed test uses α = 0.10. How much area is placed in each tail?~0.10~0.05~0.20~0.01~1~A two-tailed α splits evenly, so each tail gets α/2.','At α = 0.05 two-tailed, which pair of Z values marks the rejection boundary?~±1.645~±1.96~±2.576~±1.28~1~±1.96 leaves 2.5% in each tail, totaling α = 0.05.','At α = 0.01 two-tailed, which pair of Z values marks the rejection boundary?~±1.96~±2.326~±2.576~±1.645~2~±2.576 leaves 0.5% in each tail, totaling α = 0.01.','If α is lowered from 0.05 to 0.01, what happens to how hard it is to reject H₀?~It becomes easier~It becomes harder~No change~H₀ can never be rejected~1~A smaller α narrows the rejection region, demanding stronger evidence.','A p-value of 0.02 is obtained at α = 0.05. What is the correct decision?~Reject H₀~Fail to reject H₀~Redo the study~Change H₀ to H₁~0~Since p ≤ α, the result is significant enough to reject H₀.','After failing to reject H₀, why is "H₀ is proven true" an incorrect statement?~Failing to reject only shows insufficient evidence against H₀, not proof~H₀ is always false~Failing to reject means H₁ is proven~α becomes 0 in that case~0~A non-significant result cannot prove H₀; it only reflects a lack of strong contrary evidence.','What should be the very first step of any hypothesis test?~Compute the test statistic~State H₀ and H₁ clearly~Write the final conclusion~Choose the sample size~1~Hypotheses must be defined before any calculation or decision.','For x̄ = 52, μ₀ = 50, σ = 6, n = 36, what is the correct Z statistic?~0.33~2.00~12.00~1.00~1~Z = (52 − 50) / (6/√36) = 2 / 1 = 2.00.','Why would a researcher choose a t-test instead of a Z-test for a small sample?~σ is unknown and estimated using the sample standard deviation s~The sample size is enormous~α must be exactly 0.05~r is negative~0~t procedures are used when the population σ is unknown and s is used in its place.','Keeping the same difference x̄ − μ₀ but doubling the sample size n, what happens to the standard error?~It decreases~It increases~It stays the same~It becomes 0~0~SE = σ/√n, so a larger n produces a smaller standard error.','A computed Z = 2.20 is compared with a critical value of 1.96 in a two-tailed test. What is the decision?~Reject H₀~Fail to reject H₀~No decision possible~Accept H₀ as true~0~2.20 exceeds 1.96, placing it in the rejection region.','Why is a written conclusion that only says "reject H₀" considered incomplete in applied research?~It should be restated in the real-world context of the problem~Because H₀ should never be rejected~Because Z scores are never reported~Because α must be recalculated~0~A complete conclusion translates the statistical decision into what it means for the actual question studied.','Two variables show r = 0.90. What does this indicate about their relationship?~A strong positive linear relationship~A weak linear relationship~No relationship~A perfect negative relationship~0~r near +1 signals a strong positive linear association.','A study finds r = −0.75 between commute time and job satisfaction. What is the most accurate interpretation?~As commute time increases, job satisfaction tends to strongly decrease~Commute time has no effect on satisfaction~Longer commutes always cause unhappiness~The relationship is perfectly linear~0~A strong negative r shows an inverse linear tendency, but not proof of causation.','A report claims "more firefighters at a fire cause more damage" because both rise together. What is the best statistical criticism?~A third variable, fire severity, likely explains both, so correlation is not causation~The correlation must actually be negative~r cannot be computed for this example~Firefighters always cause damage~0~A confounding variable (fire size) explains why two unrelated-by-cause variables move together.','Which value of r shows the weakest linear relationship?~r = 0.03~r = 0.65~r = −0.80~r = 0.99~0~Values of r near 0 indicate weak or negligible linear association.','A scatter plot shows points tightly clustered along an upward line. What does this suggest about r?~r is close to +1~r is close to −1~r is close to 0~r cannot be determined~0~A tight upward pattern indicates a strong positive linear relationship.','Why is Pearson’s r a poor summary for a strong U-shaped relationship between two variables?~r only captures the strength of a linear pattern, not curves~r is always negative for curved data~U-shaped data cannot have numeric variables~r automatically equals 1 for curves~0~A curved, non-linear relationship can produce a low r even when a strong pattern exists.','A regression line is Y = 4X + 15, where X is hours practiced and Y is score. What does 15 represent?~The predicted score at 0 hours practiced~The increase in score per hour practiced~The maximum score possible~The correlation coefficient~0~The intercept is the predicted Y when X = 0.','Using Y = 4X + 15, what is the predicted score for a student who practices 5 hours?~35~20~19~24~0~Y = 4(5) + 15 = 35.','Why is it risky to use a line of best fit built from X-values between 0 and 10 to predict Y at X = 100?~That is extrapolation far beyond the observed data range~The slope becomes negative automatically~Regression lines cannot exceed X = 10~The intercept always changes sign~0~Predicting well beyond the data used to build the line risks an inaccurate extrapolation.','A regression line has a negative slope between study distractions and exam score. What does this mean?~As distractions increase, predicted exam score decreases~As distractions increase, predicted exam score increases~Distractions have no predicted effect~The intercept must be negative too~0~A negative slope means the response variable is predicted to fall as the predictor rises.','Two regression lines share the same slope but different intercepts. What does this mean graphically?~The lines are parallel, one shifted vertically above the other~The lines cross at the origin~One line is steeper than the other~The lines must be identical~0~Equal slopes with different intercepts produce parallel lines offset vertically.','A line of best fit has a slope very close to 0. What does this suggest about X and Y?~Y barely changes as X changes~Y changes dramatically with X~X and Y are perfectly correlated~The intercept must also be 0~0~A near-zero slope indicates very little linear change in Y as X increases.'].map(P);
window.EASC_WH=WH;window.EASC_Q6=Q6;
const th=document.createElement('style');th.textContent='.easc-part{margin:18px 0;padding:18px;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:14px;background:var(--panel)}.easc-part h2{color:var(--cyan2);margin:0 0 12px;font-size:16px;letter-spacing:.08em}.easc-part h3.sub2{color:var(--cyan);font-size:13px;margin:16px 0 8px}.easc-part p{line-height:1.7;font-size:13px;margin:0 0 10px}.wq{margin:12px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px}.wq label{display:block;margin:6px 0;cursor:pointer;font-size:13px}.wfb{margin-top:8px;font-size:12px;line-height:1.6}.wfb.g{color:var(--good)}.wfb.b{color:var(--bad)}';document.head.appendChild(th);
function qz(id,items,onDone){return `<div id="${id}">${items.map((it,i)=>`<div class="wq"><b>${i+1}. ${it.q}</b>${it.o.map((o,j)=>`<label><input type="radio" name="${id}_${i}" value="${j}"> ${String.fromCharCode(65+j)}) ${o}</label>`).join('')}<div class="wfb" id="${id}_fb${i}"></div></div>`).join('')}<button class="btn" onclick="EASC_submit('${id}')">SUBMIT</button><div class="wfb" id="${id}_res" style="font-size:14px;margin-top:10px"></div></div>`}
const REG=window.EASC_REG={};
window.EASC_submit=function(id){const r=REG[id];let s=0;r.items.forEach((it,i)=>{const c=document.querySelector(`input[name="${id}_${i}"]:checked`);const fb=document.getElementById(`${id}_fb${i}`);const ok=c&&+c.value===it.a;if(ok)s++;fb.className='wfb '+(ok?'g':'b');fb.innerHTML=(ok?'✓ Correct. ':(c?'✗ Incorrect. ':'✗ Unanswered. '))+`Correct answer: ${String.fromCharCode(65+it.a)}) ${it.o[it.a]}. <i>${it.w}</i>`});const pct=Math.round(s/r.items.length*100);document.getElementById(id+'_res').innerHTML=`Score: ${s}/${r.items.length} (${pct}%)`;r.done&&r.done(pct)};
